---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PersonTree from '@daviddef/archive-kit/components/PersonTree.astro';
import '@daviddef/archive-kit/styles/persontree.css';
import subjects from '../data/page-subjects.json';
import { familyOf } from '../lib/kin.js';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

/* The join this page is about, drawn. He is LIVING, so the chart shows his name,
   his two parents and no date of his own — which is the same thing the panel
   above already says in words, in the idiom the other six archives use. */
const subject = subjects.subjects.derrick;
const fam = familyOf(subject.id, { livingSelf: true });
---
<Base title="Lynn Derrick Luwinski"
  subtitle="Living. Named, and nothing more."
  lede="This archive is organised around him and tells almost nothing about him. That is deliberate.">

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>What is published here</h3>
    <dl class="kv">
      <dt>Name</dt><dd>Lynn Derrick Luwinski</dd>
      <dt>Father</dt><dd>Kurt Israel Luwinski &mdash; of record, and the man who raised him.
        <strong>Not his biological father</strong>: <a href={base + '/dna/'}>the DNA excludes him</a>,
        and who the biological father was is <a href={base + '/worklist/'}>still open</a>.</dd>
      <dt>Mother</dt><dd>Ivone de Figueiredo</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="small muted">Nothing further. No address, no occupation, no photograph, no descendants.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The join</h2>
    <PersonTree always self={fam.self} parents={fam.parents} spouses={fam.spouses}
                children={fam.children} siblings={fam.siblings}
                labels={{ parents: 'His parents' }}>
      <Fragment slot="notes">
        <p class="warn">Two lines meet here and go no further. <strong>Nothing below him is
        drawn</strong> — not because nothing is known, but because he is living and this
        archive publishes a living person's name and their relationship to the dead, and
        nothing else. Kurt Israel Luwinski is drawn here as his father
        <strong>of record and of upbringing</strong>, which is what he was.
        <strong>He is not his biological father and that is not in dispute</strong> &mdash; the
        <a href={base + '/dna/'}>DNA excludes him twice over</a>, 0.0% Ashkenazi where a quarter
        would be required and a Y haplogroup that is North Sea rather than Ashkenazi.
        <strong>What is open is who the biological father was</strong>, not whether it was Kurt.
        And the family has since said what no document here could: <em>Kurt raised him.</em>
        <a href={base + '/tree/#twolines'}>The tree draws both lines.</a></p>
      </Fragment>
    </PersonTree>
  </section>

  <h2>Why a living man is the centre of an archive that will not describe him</h2>
  <p>
    Because he is the join. Every line this archive follows runs through him and stops there. His father's
    line goes back to a Berlin tenement and a transport to Auschwitz. His mother's goes back to a
    Portuguese colonial town on the Indian Ocean. Neither of those families knew the other existed until
    1948, and they were finished with each other by 1951.
  </p>
  <p>
    The policy of the sibling archives is that living people are omitted from the build entirely — not
    hidden, not gated, not present in the output. Here that rule is bent exactly once and no further:
    Derrick is <em>named</em>, because an archive about his ancestry that refused to say whose ancestry
    it was would be absurd. Everything else about him is left out.
  </p>

  <h2>What is known and not published</h2>
  <p>
    A good deal. The source tree records more, and the research file holds more still — including the
    DNA that prompted the central finding of this archive. It stays in the private notes. If Derrick
    wishes any of it published, or wishes his name removed as well, either is done on asking, without
    argument and without requiring a reason.
  </p>

  <h2>His own paternal line, read at last</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>R-S11477 <span class="tag doc">14 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      Everything else in this archive is about people who left documents. This is the one fact that
      came from Derrick himself, and it is the oldest thing in the collection: an unbroken line of
      fathers reaching back to a single man who lived around <strong>200 BCE</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The haplogroup is <strong>R-S11477</strong> — R1b, branch <strong>U106/Z381</strong>, the North
      Sea lineage that peaks in the Netherlands and runs at about a fifth of the men of England and
      Germany. It is not a lineage characteristic of Ashkenazi Jewish paternal descent, and it is
      therefore <strong>not the line of Jacob Leibholz</strong>, who was deported from Berlin to
      Auschwitz on 26 February 1943.
    </p>
    <p>
      The archive had named this test, in writing, as the one that would break its central finding or
      confirm it. It confirmed it. The autosomal estimate said 0.0% Ashkenazi where a quarter was
      required; the Y-chromosome now says the paternal line runs to the North Sea. Two independent
      systems, no contradiction, and they agree on where as well as on what: his closest matches are an
      English family with a branch in Johannesburg, and his haplogroup's largest European population is
      <strong>England</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>What it does not do is name anyone.</strong> It cannot be compared with Jacob's own
      Y-chromosome, because Jacob's male line has ended — Siegfried's only son died in 2005, Samuel and
      Oskar were murdered without sons, and Kurt's only son of record is Derrick. The finding rests on
      the character of the lineage, not on a match. <a href={base + '/dna/'}>The full reasoning, and its limits →</a>
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    So the Berlin family is his by name, by law and by upbringing, and by the paperwork of a marriage
    in 1948 — and the line of fathers written into his chromosome goes somewhere else entirely. The
    archive holds both, and does not pretend either one cancels the other.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>He was searching for the surname twenty-five years before this archive existed
      <span class="tag doc">27 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      Among his mother&rsquo;s papers, faxed from Portugal in the week she died, is a page that has
      nothing to do with her. <strong>It is a Lycos Genealogy search for LUWINSKI in the German
      telephone directories, run on 10 June 2001</strong>, printed at the time and kept. It travelled
      to Portugal in 2010 with her birth and death certificates.
    </p>
    <figure class="doc-scan">
      <img src={base + '/luwinski-lycos-2001.jpg'} alt="A printout from Lycos Genealogy dated June 2001, listing six Luwinski entries in the German telephone directories" />
      <figcaption class="small muted">
        <strong>Lycos Genealogy, &ldquo;Germany Telephone and Address Listings&rdquo;, 10 June 2001.</strong>
        Combined matches: <strong>16</strong>. The surviving page shows the first six; the other ten
        were not photographed and are worth having if the rest of the printout survives. The fax
        timestamp of 16 March 2010 is across the top, the same transmission as the Portuguese papers.
        <strong>Everyone named is deceased</strong>, confirmed by the family. The dialling codes are
        published because they locate the households; the subscriber digits are blacked out because
        German numbers are reassigned and a twenty-five-year-old line now reaches a stranger.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      <strong>Three of the six are in Duisburg and all three are on the 0203 code</strong> — Kurt,
      Rainer and Z. — with Alfons at Bochum forty kilometres away, so four of six sit in the Ruhr.
      A rare surname clustering in one steel city is the shape of a family that moved together, and
      Duisburg took large numbers of East Prussian and Silesian expellees after 1945. The Berlin
      one is Christa, at Afrikanische Straße 32. <strong>One of the Duisburg six is called Kurt</strong>
      — a different man entirely, and no connection is claimed; it is recorded because the
      coincidence will occur to the next reader as it occurred to this one.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      It is here because it is this archive&rsquo;s own prehistory. <strong>The question being worked
      on these pages is one he asked first</strong>, on a dial-up portal, and then kept the answer to
      for a quarter of a century.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The two families that meet in him</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>There are three, and the distinction matters more here than anywhere else in this archive.</strong>
    His mother is not in dispute on any reading. His father is two people: the man the DNA points to,
    and the man whose name he carries.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Kurt Israel Luwinski is excluded as his biological father</strong> &mdash; 0.0% Ashkenazi
    where a quarter would be required, and a Y haplogroup that is North Sea rather than Ashkenazi.
    That is this archive&rsquo;s central finding. <strong>It does not make Kurt&rsquo;s family less
    his.</strong> Derrick carries their name, this archive is named for it, and the dead it has read
    most closely are theirs &mdash; Jacob Leibholz, his grandfather of record, deported from Berlin to
    Auschwitz in February 1943, and his brothers Samuel and Oskar after him.
  </p>
  <p>
    Kurt&rsquo;s own name is part of that story. <strong>He carried his mother&rsquo;s surname,
    Luwinski, and not his father&rsquo;s, Leibholz</strong>; his brother Siegfried kept Leibholz. A
    Catholic East Prussian name on a half-Jewish child in Nazi Berlin is not a clerical accident.
    <strong>The name Derrick carries was already a shelter before it was an inheritance.</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>And the family has answered the part the papers could not.</strong>
    <em>&ldquo;Kurt raised Derrick.&rdquo;</em> &mdash; David Defranceski, his son-in-law,
    28 September 2026. So the man the DNA rules out is the man who brought him up, and this archive
    now says both instead of neither.
  </p>
  <p class="small muted">
    The court file made that look unlikely and does not actually contradict it. Kurt sued Ivone in
    1948 and the child was born after he had filed &mdash; but the action was for <strong>restitution
    of conjugal rights</strong> and was granted only in <strong>1951</strong>, which in South African
    practice was the first step of a divorce rather than the end of a marriage. The marriage ran on
    paper for about three years and the child was born inside it. <strong>A filing date is not a
    departure date.</strong>
    <a href={base + '/tree/#twolines'}>The tree sets the two lines out side by side</a>, graded on the
    same ladder, because descent is not the only thing a family is.
  </p>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card"><a href={base + '/kurt/'}>His father of record</a><p>Kurt Israel Luwinski, born Berlin 1912, in Johannesburg by 1939, died there in 1968.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><a href={base + '/ivone/'}>His mother</a><p>Ivone de Figueiredo, born Lourenço Marques 4 March 1922, died Vila Nova de Gaia 14 March 2010. Her birth entry names her parents and all four of her grandparents.</p></div>
  </div>
</Base>
